    "narrative": "For July 25, 2026, **James Wood** (76.28) and **Austin Riley** (73.69) anchor the Ghost Ride the WHIP lineup as our most potent assets. **James Wood** benefits significantly from a favorable matchup against Bratt (5.90 xERA), with his leadoff spot boosting his run-scoring potential, while **Austin Riley** exploits a massive +16% park factor adjustment against Brandon Young, setting up a high-ceiling performance. **Brice Turang** also remains an elite play at 72.70; despite his pending lineup status, the projection of a #3 hole appearance against Feltner (4.93 xERA) maximizes his stolen base opportunities, which remain the primary focus of our roster construction given the scarcity of speed. **Corbin Carroll** (65.34) remains a must-start not just for his leadoff efficiency, but because the opposing pitcher's slow delivery specifically enhances his already elite stolen base floor.\n\nStrategic benching remains vital for preserving our game caps, with **Isaac Paredes** (52.31) serving as the most difficult omission. While **Isaac Paredes** provides solid utility, he fell just short of the efficiency required to unseat **Josh Naylor** (52.93) or **Gabriel Moreno** (51.75). The decision to start **Josh Naylor** over **Isaac Paredes** was driven by the optimizer\u2019s preference for **Josh Naylor**\u2019s dynamic platoon advantage against Eovaldi, despite Eovaldi\u2019s respectable 3.42 xERA. Risks for today center largely on our \"Lineup Pending\" designations for **Brice Turang**, **Austin Riley**, **Mickey Moniak**, **Jose Caballero**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, and **Josh Naylor**. Any shift in their assumed batting order\u2014specifically for **Jose Caballero**, who carries a penalty for his projected #8 spot\u2014could necessitate a move to the bench to protect our cap if their efficiency scores crater mid-day.",
